   description: "The solution allows you to use a web app to enter the associated spatial experience and read and modify aspects of the world. 
     The endpoint provides a single discrete world with some aspects of spatial data management. The main purpose is to join, connect and share the spatial world.\n\n\
     The key words \"MUST\", \"MUST NOT\", \"REQUIRED\", \"SHALL\", \"SHALL NOT\", \"SHOULD\", \"SHOULD NOT\", \"RECOMMENDED\", \"NOT RECOMMENDED\", \"MAY\", and \"OPTIONAL\" in this document are to be interpreted as described in BCP 14 [RFC2119] [RFC8174] when, and only when, they appear in all capitals, as shown here.\n\n\
-    Frames. All coordinates MUST be in meters, right-handed, +Y up. A Spatial's GeoPose anchors its origin. All other positions are local to their parent."
+    Frames. All coordinates MUST be in meters, right-handed, +Y up. A Spatial's GeoPose anchors its origin. All other positions are local to their parent.\n\n\
+    Media type. Resources MUST be served as application/wow+json, a working name that stays provisional until the group decides it. It is JSON, and its fragment identifiers are JSON Pointers (RFC 6901, section 6). application/json MUST stay available by content negotiation.\n\n\
+    Entity tags. GET /wow/world, GET /wow/spatial/{spatialID} and the node GET MUST return ETag and Last-Modified, and MUST answer a matching If-None-Match with 304. The node PUT and DELETE MUST honor If-Match and MUST answer 412, changing nothing, when it does not match (RFC 9110).\n\n\
+    Cross-origin access. Responses MUST carry Access-Control-Allow-Origin as the world's policy allows, and Access-Control-Expose-Headers: ETag. Preflight responses MUST allow the methods GET, HEAD, POST, PUT and DELETE, and the request headers Authorization, Content-Type, If-None-Match and If-Match."
